===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|fr|industrialisation}}. Analyzable as {{af|en|industrial|-ization}} or {{af|en|industrialize|-ation}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Flame, not lame-industrialisation.wav|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪʃən|s=7}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}} {{lb|en|American spelling|Oxford British spelling}}

# A process of social and economic change whereby a human society is transformed from a [[preindustrial]] to an [[industrial]] state.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=E. K. Hunt|title=Property and Prophets|year=1990|publisher=Harper & Row|location=New York|edition=6th|isbn=0-06-043003-6|oclc=20295502|passage=It was during this period of  '''industrialization''' that the individualistic world view of classical liberalism became the dominant ideology of capitalism.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1996|author=Armand Mattelart|title=The Invention of Communication
|passage={{...}} between two periods within the era of '''industrialization''': the "paleotechnic" under the sway of steam and mechanics, that coincides with the "imperial-financial age," the era of Kriegspiel or "wardom"; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Rhea Mogul|title=This megacity is the latest to shut down as pollution chokes swathes of South Asia|magazine=w:CNN|url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/11/10/asia/pakistan-india-pollution-new-delhi-lahore-intl-hnk/index.html|date=2023-11-10|passage=The PM2.5 levels in all these cities far exceed the World Health Organization’s limit and illustrate a growing concern for South Asian countries as they experience rapid '''industrializations''' and population booms that are fueling pollution levels.}}

